# Headers for the static files Workers serves without running the Worker.
#
# Workers' static assets use the same `_headers` syntax Netlify did, so these are the three
# `[[headers]]` blocks netlify.toml carried, with the same values and the same reasons. The
# Worker applies the same CSP to the responses it generates itself; workers/response-headers.ts
# holds that copy, and workers/__tests__/response-headers.test.ts fails if the two disagree.

# The staleness probe in buildFreshness.ts compares this file's id against the one baked into the
# running bundle. A cached response would echo the tab's own build back to it forever, so the
# banner would never appear.
/build-id.json
  Cache-Control: no-store, max-age=0

# The one file that must never be answered from cache, for the same reason as the one above — and
# the pair belongs together. /build-id.json is how staleness is DETECTED; this is how it gets
# RESOLVED. The browser discovers a new worker only by re-fetching /sw.js, so under a long
# max-age that check can be satisfied by an old copy while the installed worker keeps serving its
# precached build. On 2026-08-24 a Safari session was pinned to a build whose /assets/* had all
# been deleted, running that dead code until it threw. Protecting only the detector is the worst
# of both — the banner appears, and the refresh behind it re-serves the very build it is warning
# about.
#
# `max-age=0, must-revalidate` rather than `no-store`: it still forces a check on every request,
# but lets the ETag answer 304 instead of resending the file.
/sw.js
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate

